# CTU with PV = 0.
#
# What the standard says: the CTU body (ed.3 table 44) ends with
# Q := (CV >= PV); with PV = 0 and CV starting at 0 this gives Q = TRUE
# from the very first evaluation, before any count and also right after a
# reset. So the body, read literally, defines this case. It is kept under
# `undefined/` because several commercial runtimes treat PV = 0 as
# "disabled" (Q never TRUE) or as "Q on first count", and because the
# standard never discusses PV = 0 explicitly; a suite that wants to be
# strict can promote this file to `counters/` with `status: conformant`.
#
# What IronPLC 0.251.0 (5ea0610) does: the literal body, Q = TRUE always.
# Recorded 2026-10-31.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: cu r
outputs: q cv
status: implementation-defined
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 6.6.3.5.3 table 44 (CTU body)
---
0 cu=FALSE r=FALSE => q=TRUE cv=0
1 cu=TRUE => q=TRUE cv=1
2 cu=FALSE => q=TRUE cv=1
3 cu=TRUE => q=TRUE cv=2
4 r=TRUE => q=TRUE cv=0
5 r=FALSE cu=FALSE => q=TRUE cv=0
